+## Safety rails on load
+
+Active guards in front of every run. They are decided in code and enforced in the runner
+itself, so the UI, the API and the agent's own orchestration all pass through the same gate.
+
+| Rail | Setting (`.env`) | Default | What it does |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Target allow-list | `RUN_ALLOWED_HOSTS` | empty (any host) | Load, and pre-flight requests, only go to approved hosts. Entries: `staging.example.com`, `127.0.0.1:8000` (that port only), `*.test.example.com`. |
+| Max virtual users | `RUN_MAX_USERS` | `1000` | A run that reaches more is blocked. `0` = no cap. |
+| Max duration | `RUN_MAX_DURATION_SECONDS` | `3600` | A run that lasts longer is blocked (60 s of k6 wind-down is allowed on top). `0` = no cap. |
+| Concurrent runs | `RUN_MAX_CONCURRENT` | `3` | Most runs in progress at once. `0` = no limit. |
+| Kill switch | 🛑 in the top bar, or `POST /api/safety/kill-switch` | off | Stops every run in progress and refuses new ones until released. Saved in the database, so it survives a restart. |
+
+How they are enforced:
+
+- **The caps are read from the script itself**, with `k6 inspect`, not from the plan — a
+  script can be edited or uploaded, so the plan is not proof of what will run. If a cap is
+  set and the script's size cannot be determined, the run is blocked (fail closed).
+- **The allow-list is checked twice:** the target and every URL written in the script before
+  the run, and every host actually requested during it — a request to a host that is not
+  allowed stops the run.
+- **A watchdog** stops a run that outlives the duration cap.
+- A blocked run never starts the load engine. Blocks and safety stops are in the audit log
+  (`run.blocked`, `run.safety_stop`, `safety.kill_switch_on/off`).
+
+`GET /api/safety` returns the limits in force and the kill-switch state.
+
+---
+
+## Durable runs
+
+A live run is mirrored to the database (`runs` table) when it starts, about once a second
+while it runs, and when it ends — status, metrics, logs.
+
+- **Refresh the page mid-run** and a banner offers to rejoin it (`GET /api/runs/active`).
+- **After a restart, finished runs are still there**: their live view and report open as before.
+- **A run that was in flight when the app stopped is marked `interrupted`**, keeping the
+  metrics it had. A k6 process left behind is stopped, so load never keeps firing with
+  nothing watching it. The run itself does not resume — that needs separate load workers.
+- **A run cut short is never reported as PASS.** Stopped (by a person, a safety limit or the
+  kill switch) or interrupted runs measured only part of the test: a failed threshold is
+  still a failure, but a pass becomes *inconclusive*, with the reason shown.
+
+---
+
